  "hints": [
    "Generating all 2^(2n) strings and filtering works for tiny n but wastes most of the effort.",
    "Build the string one character at a time while tracking how many \"(\" and \")\" are already placed.",
    "You may add \"(\" while fewer than n are placed, and \")\" only while fewer \")\" than \"(\" are placed. Try \"(\" before \")\" and the results come out already sorted."
  ],
  "solution": {
    "js": "function balancedStrings(n) {\n  const result = []\n  const build = (text, open, close) => {\n    if (text.length === 2 * n) {\n      result.push(text)\n      return\n    }\n    if (open < n) build(text + '(', open + 1, close)\n    if (close < open) build(text + ')', open, close + 1)\n  }\n  build('', 0, 0)\n  return result\n}\n",
    "py": "def balanced_strings(n):\n    result = []\n\n    def build(text, opened, closed):\n        if len(text) == 2 * n:\n            result.append(text)\n            return\n        if opened < n:\n            build(text + '(', opened + 1, closed)\n        if closed < opened:\n            build(text + ')', opened, closed + 1)\n\n    build('', 0, 0)\n    return result\n",
    "explanation": "The two counters restrict the search to prefixes that can still be completed, so every branch ends in a valid string and no time is spent on dead ends. Because \"(\" sorts before \")\" and is tried first, the depth-first search emits strings in sorted order."
  },
  "complexity": {
    "time": "O(n · C(n))",
    "space": "O(n) plus the output",
    "notes": "C(n) is the nth Catalan number, roughly 4^n / (n^1.5 · √π), which counts the valid strings; each costs O(n) to build."
  },
  "followUps": [
    "How would you count the valid strings without listing them?"
  ]
}
